{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "demonstration",
      "description": "At the video cycle, record the ordered list of all supplied demonstration frame labels, including the initial and final frames. Preserve this list unchanged throughout execution. These images specify the entire demonstrated route, including obstacle sides, intermediate turns, reversals, repeated visits and the stopping point; do not replace it with an unordered set of visited places. e.g. [\"V0\", \"V16\", \"V32\", \"V48\", \"V64\", \"V80\", \"V96\", \"V99\"]",
      "value_type": "keyframe",
      "tolerance": 0
    },
    {
      "name": "completed_legs",
      "description": "The number of demonstrated waypoint-to-waypoint legs already completed by the execution at the current observation, excluding the action selected at this cycle. Set to zero at the video cycle and the initial act cycle. Increment when the observed execution reaches the next demonstrated leg endpoint within 1 cm. Count intermediate bypass points as endpoints, not just marked gaps. Repeated visits count separately in chronological order. Do not increment for a motion that executes no steps, an incomplete approach, or a wait. Preserve the count otherwise. e.g. 2",
      "value_type": "count",
      "tolerance": 0
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "demonstration",
      "description": "At the video cycle, record the ordered list of all supplied demonstration frame labels, including the initial and final frames. Preserve this list unchanged throughout execution. These images specify the entire demonstrated route, including obstacle sides, intermediate turns, reversals, repeated visits and the stopping point; do not replace it with an unordered set of visited places. e.g. [\"V0\", \"V16\", \"V32\", \"V48\", \"V64\", \"V80\", \"V96\", \"V99\"]"
    },
    {
      "name": "completed_legs",
      "description": "Write zero at the video cycle and initial act cycle. At subsequent act cycles, use the incoming count to identify the next uncompleted endpoint in demonstration. Compare the current TCP and the observed motion with that endpoint. Increment by one only if the motion shown has reached it within 1 cm; otherwise preserve the count. Count bypass endpoints and repeated visits separately. An explicitly zero-step action, an incomplete approach or a wait does not advance the count. Record only arrivals already observed, never the endpoint of the action being selected now. e.g. 2"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "At the video cycle, produce memory only. At execution start, the stick is already at the demonstrated starting point: do not add a grasp, lift or approach action. Use demonstration to reconstruct the ordered planar route of the lower free end of the attached stick. Follow the actual stick tip, not the robot housing or the red marker: a red marker can remain at the previous gap while the tip travels around an obstacle. The white trail helps identify which side was traversed, but is not itself the complete task history.\n\nInfer the route's leg endpoints from successive changes of travel direction, including bypass points beside the ends of obstacles and arrivals at gaps. In these demonstrations a leg lasts approximately 25 control steps; useful endpoint times are approximately 0, 25, 50 and so on through the final frame. The frames straddle these times, so reconstruct the turning point from the adjacent incoming and outgoing trajectories rather than treating every displayed frame as a waypoint. Use the full video to distinguish retracing a bypass from continuing around the opposite end. Preserve all repetitions: reaching a previously visited gap does not mean the route is finished. Do not assume that all obstacles must be visited or that every traversal alternates sides.\n\nRecover endpoint positions geometrically using the camera calibration and the tip's plane z=0.07 m. Intersect the ray through the lower free end with that plane; do not intersect the robot hand or upper end of the stick. When the tip is partially occluded, use neighboring demonstration frames, the visible shaft, obstacle geometry and the incoming/outgoing path to infer its endpoint. Ground-level marker centers must be reconstructed on their own ground plane rather than being treated as tip-height pixels. Use current front and wrist observations to refine alignment with the same static obstacle arrangement. Colors identify obstacles within the episode but do not define a universal route.\n\nFirst evaluate whether the current observation completes the next leg, according to the completed_legs memory rule. With the resulting completed count c, command the endpoint of leg c+1. Set TCP z to 0.07 m and preserve the current approximately downward orientation, normalizing equivalent roll angles if needed. Keep the gripper closed; its command is ignored for this stick tool. Command the demonstrated full leg endpoint, typically about 12 cm away, rather than inserting arbitrary small subdivisions, lifting over an obstacle, or moving directly across an obstacle to a later gap. Accept normal arrival error within 1 cm and move on instead of issuing a redundant correction to a reached endpoint.\n\nIf the expected endpoint has not been reached, retain the count and complete that leg. If localization is ambiguous, use the retained video's neighboring frames and the current wrist view to select the endpoint consistent with both route order and obstacle clearance; never choose a later occurrence merely because it is closer. If a direct recovery would intersect an obstacle, make a conservative planar correction toward the demonstrated safe corridor without advancing the count until its endpoint is reached. Do not issue an identical-pose motion. If all demonstrated legs are complete and the environment has not yet terminated, hold with a short wait solely to allow terminal evaluation; do not start an extra loop."
}
